From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:05:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] colorspace: tune the peak of HLG to the target only where its
 transfer is HDR

pl_color_space_infer_map() tunes the peak of an HLG source to the
target's wherever the target counts as HDR, and pl_color_space_is_hdr()
counts an SDR target as HDR once its white is above the reference white
of SDR (203 cd/m^2). An HLG picture mapped to an SDR display set brighter
(white at 300 cd/m^2, gamma 2.2) was then shown as on an HDR display of
that peak: its scene light times 300, the system gamma clamped to 1, and
nothing tone mapped; to an SDR display of 203 cd/m^2 or less it is shown
at its nominal peak (1000 cd/m^2, ITU-R BT.2100) and tone mapped. The
brighter display got the darker picture: the light of 100 cd/m^2 of the
HLG reference display came out at 44 cd/m^2 on it, against 87 on the
display of 203 cd/m^2, and 99 for a PQ picture of the same light on the
same display.

The peak is tuned to the target only where its transfer is HDR (PQ or
HLG): HLG drawn to PQ (as Dolby Vision, or for Dolby Vision Low-Latency)
is as before, and an SDR target of any white takes HLG at its nominal
peak and maps it with the tone curve, as it does at 203 cd/m^2 and below.

Measured with Kodi's Dolby Vision bridge on Mesa's software OpenGL ES
(llvmpipe), a neutral ramp of every 10-bit code: HLG mapped to SDR at
300 cd/m^2 and gamma 2.2 within 0.12 of a 10-bit code of the BT.2100
display of 1000 cd/m^2 tone mapped by the curve (BT.2390 with
libplacebo's knee offset), 99.8 cd/m^2 for that light of 100 cd/m^2.
HLG to SDR at 203 and 100 cd/m^2, PQ to 203, 300 and 100 cd/m^2 give
the same bytes as before, and HLG sent as Dolby Vision the same files.
---
 src/colorspace.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/colorspace.c b/src/colorspace.c
index b0ac828..6f582db 100644
--- a/src/colorspace.c
+++ b/src/colorspace.c
@@ -1053,7 +1053,12 @@ void pl_color_space_infer_map(struct pl_color_space *src,
         dst->hdr.max_luma = src->hdr.max_luma;
 
     // If the src is HLG and the output is HDR, tune the HLG peak to the output
-    if (src->transfer == PL_COLOR_TRC_HLG && pl_color_space_is_hdr(dst))
+    // (Hades: an output of an HDR transfer. An SDR output whose white is above
+    // the reference white of SDR, a display set brighter, takes HLG as every
+    // other SDR output does: at its nominal peak, tone mapped. Tuned to the
+    // output, it was shown as on an HDR display of that peak, darker in the
+    // mid-tones than on the reference SDR display.)
+    if (src->transfer == PL_COLOR_TRC_HLG && pl_color_transfer_is_hdr(dst->transfer))
         src->hdr.max_luma = dst->hdr.max_luma;
 }
 
